Pressure Groups:-

A pressure group is a group of individuals who share an interest or cause and come together to put pressure on policymakers at Westminster and elsewhere. They are defined by : Seeking to exert influence from outside government, having a narrow issues focus, uniting members in a single shared belief, cause or set of interests.

Sometimes also known as NGOs (non governmental organisations)

Social Movements:- 

There are also Social movements which are different to pressure groups because they are : larger, more diffuse group of many people - no single organisation to join, aiming long-term fundamental change rather than a short-term policy objective, E.g. the civil rights movement in 1960s USA; also women’s rights; e.g. the occupy movement in the early 21st Century; environmentalism / climate change; globalisation; Black Lives Matter.

Function of pressure groups:-

1. Represent the interests of communities not fully represented in parliament
2. Increase political **[[participation
3. Protect the interests of minority groups - Stonewall
4. Promoting causes not adequately taken up by political parties
5. To inform and educate the public about political issues and government activity
6. To call government to account over performance in specific areas
7. To help politicians formulate and implement policy

How do pressure groups exert their influence:-

Method:-

- Protest 
- E-Petition
- Campaigning
- Online campaigns
- Media
- Strikes
- Judicial review
- Civil disobedience
- Funding
- Lobbying
- Public research
- Parliamentary committees

Access Points:-

- MP 
- Councillor
- Social media
- Parliament
- Ministers, civil servants
- Public opinion
- Courts
- Direct action







